  "notes": "Detroit's quiet-storm-jazz end of the lane, \"working almost entirely below 90 BPM\" — the entry's 62–92 band is the narrowest and slowest of the modern models here. `confidence` is **low**: the research rates it medium-low and says only the self-production and the lane position are sourced, with \"tempo/harmonic detail inference\". The drum instruction is a mix instruction — \"soft, brushed or programmed-quiet kit with a swung 16th hat, snare mixed low on 2&4, and a continuous shaker; the drum layer is deliberately unobtrusive so the keyboard and sax carry the record\" — so the velocity tiers are soft, the kick is 2–3 a bar at low syncopation, the hat is continuous but quiet, and the shaker is the busiest lane in the kit. Harmony is \"maj7/maj9 tonics, ii–V–I turnarounds, and modal interchange on the bridge; flat keys dominate\". Melody is \"soft high-tenor at very low volume and low note-density, phrases long and legato, almost no melisma\", which is the legato articulation, density 2–5, the long-arc structure and a very high chord-tone bias. The countermelody instrument is named — \"soprano/alto sax as the countermelody instrument\" — so the counter answers at +12. Bass is \"round fretless-sounding electric bass with slides between chord tones, long notes, and a smooth-jazz register (E1–G2)\", which is why the register stops at 43 and `glideProb` is the highest of any bass in this batch. ⚠ Every drum and melody number here is INFERRED, and so is the second progression family: the entry names \"modal interchange on the bridge\", and the borrowed I–iv is this author's reading of that phrase rather than a numeral it wrote.",
